@meta
id: 03-02
title: Insert in front of the code, not the indent
chapter: 03
type: lesson
skills: insert-I
par: 18
minutes: 4
vscode: click just before `this`, type `await `
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
The refund call is not awaited, so a failed refund is swallowed and the
order is cancelled anyway. Put `await ` in front of it.

The line is indented four spaces. What you want is the first character of
the actual code, not column one — those are different places, and only one
of them is where the word belongs.

This is the mirror image of the last lesson. There, you wanted the far end
of the line. Here you want the near end, but the useful near end.

@keys
| `i` | insert to the left of the cursor |
| `0` | column one, indent and all |
| `^` | the first character that is not a space |
| `I` | insert at the first non-blank character |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
`A` had a partner. If `A` is "append at the end of the line", there is a
capital that means "insert at the start of the code on this line".
--
`I` puts you in insert mode in front of the first non-blank character —
after the indent, not before it. That is nearly always what you meant.
--
Get down to the refund line with `j`, then `I`, type `await ` with its
trailing space, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    j j I await<Space> <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`I` is `^i` in one key, and `^` — first non-blank — is the version of
"start of line" that a programmer actually wants. `0` would have put you
before the indent, and `await ` would have landed in the whitespace,
breaking the indentation for no reason.

`I` and `A` are a pair, and they are the two keys that make "move to where
I want to type, then type" collapse into one step. Between them they cover
most single-line edits you will make this year: prefix something, or
terminate something.

Notice what they save you. Not keystrokes — two or three each time. What
they save is the decision about *where the cursor is*. You stop thinking in
columns.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    this.paymentService.refund(id);
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    await this.paymentService.refund(id);
  }
}
